// Golden cartridge: a compact reference for writing a complete Dramatoric story
// game in one .drama play.
//
// `./talky lint golden` flags every field the engine would silently ignore.
// Lint after every edit.
// The complete value vocabularies (functions, poses, gestures, effect fields,
// event verbs) are in registry.md beside this file — generated from the
// engine, so it is exhaustive: names not listed there do not exist.
//
// Syntax primer
//
// IDs:
// - Use snake_case ids: cartridge ids, prefab names, entity ids, action ids,
//   cue ids.
// - IDs are normalized to lowercase snake_case. Do not use spaces or dots.
// - Entity ids are flat and global. Use `<child> parent <owner>` to build trees.
// - Labels and verbs are display text. Title Case is a good style.
// - Keep `Action verb` and `Action on` spelling/case the same, such as Press.
//
// Prefabs and cues:
// - There is no static scene. Prefabs are entity templates; cues instantiate
//   them at runtime with `Stage add` lines and tear them down with
//   `Stage remove`.
// - The boot cue (top-level `boot`, default "boot") assembles the world. It is
//   fast-forwarded to completion whenever a world is built, so it must only
//   add/remove prefabs and apply instant directions — no waits or dialogue.
// - `Scene start opening` is just a cue named "opening". It does not auto-run
//   by name.
// - Start a cue from a host option, or during play with `World cue <id>`.
//
// Scripts:
// - `{{ ... }}` means interpolate text or a value inside a string.
// - Formula-only fields contain CEL directly. In an effect or ordinary value
//   position, `=` introduces CEL instead of a literal.
// - HUD text and HUD `for` loops update live, several times per second.
// - `face.*` expressions update live, several times per second.
// - `desc` templates render when someone looks at the entity.
// - Action `when` runs when action choices are built.
// - Action `observable` and `react` run when the event is made.
// - Listener `when` expressions run when a matching observed event happens.
//
// Useful expression functions:
// - distance(a,b): distance in scene units.
// - visible(a,b): true when b can notice a — near enough, present, and with no
//   solid scenery (a shut door, a wall) between them.
// - held(item, holder): true when holder carries item.
// - emotion(id,name): reads a public 0..1 state value.
// - knows(id,text): true when knowledge contains text, ignoring case.
// - actors(): visible, non-player NPCs as {id, name}.
// - silence(): seconds since anyone spoke. idle(id): seconds since id was last
//   in the action — acting, or being spoken to or acted upon.
// - quietest(): id of the visible minded NPC who has held its tongue longest —
//   lets one pulse reaction elect a single speaker instead of a pile-up.
// - clamp(x,min,max), chance(p), randint(min,max), dice(sides), roll(n,sides),
//   pick(list).
//
// Script names:
// - `actor`: entity doing the action. In a listener, the listener's owner.
// - `target`: primary entity acted on, such as the clicked object.
// - `object`: secondary entity. For Say, this is the addressed listener.
// - `observer`: entity being tested for noticing or reacting.
// - `cause`: event that triggered a listener. It has seq, verb, actor, value,
//   and its roles, including target, object, and knowledge provenance from.
// - `looker`: entity reading a description line.
// - `self`: in a desc template, the described entity — {id, name} plus the
//   humanoid description fields listed under Naturalism below.
// - `it`: current item in a HUD `for` row.
// - `player`: current player entity, available in `face:` expressions.
//
// Naturalism — write only what the engine can vouch for:
// - Never author a claim the world can contradict. Position, posture, gaze,
//   mood, and dress are live state; interpolate them instead of imagining
//   them. A humanoid's desc can read {{self.look}} — the full appearance in
//   one clause — or the parts: {{self.species}}, {{self.skin}}, {{self.eyes}},
//   {{self.hair}}, {{self.build}}, {{self.face}}, {{self.wearing}},
//   {{self.posture}}, {{self.mood}}, {{self.watching}}, plus every raw
//   structure knob ({{self.jaw_squareness}}). "Sitting on the sofa" is a lie
//   the moment she stands; {{self.posture}} never lies. The same truths are
//   readable anywhere via humanoid(id), e.g. {{humanoid('ana').watching}}.
// - A desc is camera truth: what an observer sees this instant. Timeless
//   physical traits are fine; interiority, backstory, and imagery carrying no
//   scene information ("checking her reflection in his eyes") are not. Never
//   presume a relationship with {{looker.name}} — the looker may be anyone.
// - A mind knows only what it lived or was told. Its own past belongs in
//   prompt and `knows` lines. Anything about another character must be
//   discovered in play: gate attitude passages on knows()/heardFrom() so
//   contempt or fear appears only after its cause was actually said, and let
//   Shared/Learned carry facts from mouth to mouth.
// - A secret a model holds is a secret a model telegraphs. If a reveal must
//   wait, exclude it from the prompt until its time: prompts re-render every
//   turn, and {{#if expr}}...{{elseif expr}}...{{else}}...{{/if}} include a
//   passage only when its condition holds. For "later in the story", ratchet
//   a hidden story entity's emotion from a listener and gate on it.
// - Write wants, not world-claims: "make her look at you" is a goal that is
//   always true; "she will not look at you" is a claim that is usually false.
// - Reference only affordances that exist: a desc that mentions sitting needs
//   a collidable support surface with a seated pose; a prompt that mentions a
//   prop needs the prop.
// games/live/noexit exercises all of these.
//
// Actions:
// - A player/NPC command action has `verb`, optional `label`, optional `when`,
//   optional `observable`, repeatable `react`, and ordered effect lines.
// - `label` is display text. If omitted, the verb is used.
// - `verb` creates an action option when `when` is true.
// - `when` answers: can this action happen?
// - `observable` answers: who notices after it happens? If omitted, nearby NPCs
//   notice.
// - `react` scores who may speak next. It is priority, not probability.
//   Defaults are actor: 0, target: 1, object: 1, observer: 0.
//   false or 0 suppresses that role. Higher numbers win.
// - A listener action has `Action on Verb`. It is not shown as a player option.
//   When its owner observes a matching event and `when` is true, its effects run.
// - `Action on Pulse` listens to the ambient metronome (`Game pulse`), the way
//   to act on dead air: nothing happening is not an event, so the pulse is.
//
// Effects:
// - Each line inside an Action block is one ordered effect.
// - `Actor`, `Target`, `Object`, and `Observer` are event roles. Other subjects
//   are literal entity ids; `World` addresses stagecraft globals.
// - Literal arguments need no marker: `Actor gesture nod`.
// - Formula arguments use `=`: `Target watch = actor`.
// - Writable entity fields: name, desc, controller, hear, seek,
//   support, watch, hidden, perceivable, pose, gesture, turn, pos, rot, place, attach, detach,
//   humanoid, and emotion.<name>. Multiple arguments stay ordered and typed.
// - Writable World verbs are camera, camera_follow, control, cue, creator, add,
//   and remove.
//
// NPC mind fields:
// - `prompt`: core persona, wants, and speaking rules. Prompt lines
//   accumulate, so identity and wants can ride on separate lines.
// - `knows` lines: private facts. Use for stable facts, rules, clues —
//   and, via the `hear` effect, things that happen in play.
// - Knowledge is shown to the NPC on its turn.
// - `knows(id,text)` checks knowledge by substring.
// - `emotion`: public numeric state. Known face names also drive the face.
//   Add duration seconds for a cooling moodlet: `Target emotion.angry 0.7 12s`.
//   Timed contributions stack; omit duration for durable story state.
// - `face`: live derived emotion values.
// - `pose`, `gesture`, and `watch`: visible body and attention state.
// - `seek`: movement intent.
// - `turn`: asks an NPC to respond to an event.
//
// Staging, props, and zones:
// - `anchor` marks a useful place. A pose on a collidable surface declares a
//   supported posture; it is not an authored body-root position.
// - `seek anchor_id` walks to that mark and faces its authored direction.
// - `support chair_surface` approaches, solves the posed body against the
//   object's colliders, and applies its pose/watch.
// - `place` puts a prop on an anchor. `attach` puts a prop on an entity socket.
// - `detach` releases a prop at its current world position.
// - `zone` marks an invisible area. Use `in_zone(actor, zone)` in conditions.
// - Moving into or out of a zone creates Enter and Exit events for listeners.
// - Staying settled inside a zone creates Inside events about once per second.
//
// Cues:
// - A cue is a named script opened with `Scene start <id>`.
// - End every cue with `Scene end`; a scene id is declared exactly once.
// - A cue may be one list of lines, or contain `Track start <id>` /
//   `Track end` blocks for parallel work.
// - Tracks start together and run in parallel.
// - `wait` pauses only its own track.
// - `sync` gives tracks a named meeting point. A track waits there
//   until every other track using that same name has reached it.
// - Every line has one subject. `Stage` is the technical crew — camera, fades,
//   timing — and its `add`/`remove` verbs instantiate and tear down prefabs.
// - Entity subjects control actors: `mentor: Line` speaks; `mentor gesture nod`
//   directs. One direction per line; consecutive instant lines land in the
//   same beat.
// - Actor directions: say, pose, gesture, seek, support, watch, hidden, perceivable, place,
//   attach, detach, emotion.
// - `emotion` is `mentor emotion angry 0.5 [seconds]` — one name and level per line.
// - `seek` starts movement; it does not wait. Use `Stage arrive { ... }`.
//   `walkto`/`support` sugar emits the seek and its arrive barrier together.
// - A film scene is wrapped in capture/release automatically; a `bare` scene
//   is not — these reference cues are bare and spell the capture, cut, and
//   release lines out.
// - Without `wait`, a title/fade waits for in+hold+out, move/zoom waits for
//   secs, and speech waits long enough to read.
//
// HUD:
// - A HUD panel lives in an entity's `hud`. It is on screen while that
//   entity is live and not hidden, so panels spawn and despawn with prefabs.
// - A carrier entity needs no geometry: an invisible prefab works.
// - `anchor` is left, right, top, or bottom.
// - `HUD if <condition>` controls the whole panel.
// - Rows are text, optionally followed by `for = <list expression>`.
// - There are no per-row icons, colors, or conditions here. Put logic in the text
//   expression, or hide a whole panel with `HUD if`.
//
// Entity and metadata fields:
// - Entity fields include transform, geometry, material, collider, camera, light,
//   billboard, tags, and metadata. Declare every nested leaf as a dotted path.
// - Recognized metadata fields include name, desc, mind, hidden, perceivable, holdable,
//   controller, sockets, anchor, zone, humanoid, watch, pose, seek, face,
//   advertise, enter, and exit. Actions and surfaces use their dedicated blocks.
// - `advertise false` hides automatic context-action rows without disabling the
//   object's actions. A sole action can still be activated by clicking a pickable object.
// - `enter 0.6` / `exit 0.4` dissolve the entity in and out (seconds) on
//   spawn, reveal, and despawn. Pure presentation: the logical change is
//   instant everywhere (perception, saves, replay).
// - Knowledge (the `hear` effect) and `emotion` usually change through effects. `humanoid.expression`
//   can seed starting emotion values.
// - Unknown metadata can be notes for tools, but it does not affect play by
//   itself. For mutable global story state, prefer a hidden entity.
//
// Layout:
// - Declaration order never changes meaning (only line order inside a cue or
//   action block does), so arrange a play for the reader. The convention:
//   metadata, then engine config, then look (environment, materials, effects),
//   then characters, then the set, then interactive props, then scenes.
// - Group by dramatic subject, not statement type: keep an entity's
//   declaration, name/desc, actions, and canvas together as one stanza. Put a
//   character's generation config, pools, prefab, listeners, and casting cue
//   in one band, so "how people are made" reads in one place.
// - Indent the interior of start/end blocks two spaces; the compiler ignores
//   leading whitespace everywhere, and a fenced """ body sheds the whitespace
//   its lines share. games/live/vivarium is the worked example.

Game id golden
Game title "Golden Cartridge"
Game author "Dramatoric"
Game version "1"
Game description "Reference cartridge exercising prefabs, NPC, action, HUD, cue, and scripting affordances."

// The cue that assembles the world. "boot" is also the default, so this line
// is optional; it is spelled out here for reference.
Game boot boot

// Visual style for this cartridge. `pixel` gives the low-res Talky look.
Game render.pipeline pixel
Game render.pixelScale 2

// Movement step size in meters. Smaller lets characters stand closer together.
Game grid.cell 0.5

// The ambient metronome: about every `every` seconds of play (seed-jittered so
// ambience never feels mechanical) a Pulse event reaches every entity with
// listeners, and `Action on Pulse` reactions fill dead air — fidgets, roaming nudges,
// story-clock advances, or `turn` requests gated by silence()/quietest().
// Nonnegative `bursts` caps how many LLM turns pulses may start between player
// actions, so an unattended room settles instead of spending forever. Player
// action refills it; -1 keeps an autonomous world talking continuously. Shorthand
// `Game pulse 9` uses the default bursts. Omit `pulse` entirely for a clockwork
// cartridge: no pulses, no ambient spend.
Game pulse.every 9
Game pulse.bursts 3

// Bubble lifetime is shared by live play, authored cues, replays, and films.
// The engine uses the longer of reading time and voiced clip time, then lingers
// and fades. `Speech End` listeners fire after the fade is gone. A failed turn
// with no NPC speech emits `Dialogue End` for the intended respondent instead.
Game dialogue.lifetime.min 1.6
Game dialogue.lifetime.max 8
Game dialogue.lifetime.chars 0.055
Game dialogue.lifetime.audio 1
Game dialogue.lifetime.linger 0.35
Game dialogue.fade 0.3

// Let players use `/do`, `/as`, `/log`, etc., and see command hints.
Game ui.slashCommands true
Game ui.hints true

// Scene-wide policy: sky, lighting, and atmosphere. Not an entity. Ambient and
// sun are colors whose luminance is their strength (defaults #949494 / #616161);
// sun.direction points toward the light. Fog is linear over eye distance, its
// color defaulting to the sky horizon; far <= near (or no fog) disables it.
Game environment.sky.top "#202938"
Game environment.sky.bottom "#5a6c7a"
Game environment.ambient "#8e9499"
Game environment.sun.color "#616161"
Game environment.sun.direction [-0.35, 0.85, 0.45]
Game environment.fog { color: #4a5a66, near: 12, far: 60 }

// The shared material table every prefab draws from.
Game materials.floor.color #3b4148
Game materials.floor.roughness 0.9
Game materials.wall.color #787f88
Game materials.wall.roughness 0.85
Game materials.trim.color #b08a48
Game materials.trim.roughness 0.55
Game materials.glass.color #4aa3b8
Game materials.glass.roughness 0.2
Game materials.glass.metallic 0.1
Game materials.warm_light.color #fff1c2
Game materials.warm_light.emissive #fff1c2
Game materials.mentor.color #c98958
Game materials.mentor.shader toon
Game materials.mentor.params.bands 4
Game materials.rival.color #7f8fc9
Game materials.rival.shader toon
Game materials.rival.params.bands 4
Game materials.player.color #6fb77a
Game materials.player.shader toon
Game materials.player.params.bands 4
Game materials.paper.color #e9dfc5
Game materials.paper.roughness 0.75
Game materials.prism.color #9be7ff
Game materials.prism.roughness 0.2
Game materials.marker.color #222222

// Prefabs are entity templates the cues instantiate. An entity line outside a
// scene builds structure; its root id defaults to the entity name. Decompose
// by lifetime: one `room` prefab for everything static (named `room` here
// because `Set` is a reserved stage subject), one prefab per character, so a
// cue can add or remove any of them independently.
//
// Spatial rules:
// - Units are meters.
// - Positions are [x, y, z]. Y is up.
// - Rotation is [x, y, z] in degrees. For people, Y is the facing yaw.
// - Angular velocity is [x, y, z] in degrees/sec.
// - Yaw 0 faces +Z. This room names -Z as "north".
// - A plane lies on X/Z at its entity origin.
// - Boxes, sprites, spheres, and humanoids are centered on their origin.
//   Put a standing person at about half their height, so feet meet the floor.
// - A prefab is authored around its own origin. A bare `Stage add x` drops it
//   at that authored transform; `pos`/`rot` on the add override the root, and
//   `parent` composes it into a live entity's frame.
//
// Geometry keys:
// - plane: width, height.
// - sprite: width, height.
// - box: width, height, depth, optional skew.
// - sphere: radius or radiusX/radiusY/radiusZ, optional segments/rings.
// - cylinder: height, radius or radiusTop/radiusBottom, optional sides.
// - extrude: profile, depth.
// - lathe: profile of [radius, y] points revolved around Y, optional sides.
// - mesh: vertices, faces.
// - collider: box, sphere, mesh, or map with kind, center, size, radius.
// - light: type point/directional/spot, intensity, color, range, angle.
// - material: color, emissive, texture, roughness, metallic, shader, params.

// Personas. A `prompt`, `knows`, or `persona.*` line makes an entity a
// character with a persona of the same id; prefab structure lines follow below.
player prompt """
You are the player. You say only what the player types.
"""

mentor prompt """
You are Mentor, a calm director inside a tiny reference scene. You
know this room is an example cartridge. Stay in character anyway.
When the visitor acts, explain the consequence in plain in-world
terms.
"""
// Prompt lines accumulate: a later line appends to the same prompt, so
// wants and scenario notes can ride separately from identity text.
mentor prompt What you want: keep the demonstration calm and legible, and encourage the visitor to learn by trying actions.
// `knows` lines are what Mentor starts out knowing. Use them for facts.
// The `hear` effect can add more knowledge during play; `knows()` checks
// it by substring.
mentor knows The console opens the gate safely.
mentor knows The rival thinks the visitor may replace them.
// Every action and listener is one block. `when` guards it, `observable`
// selects witnesses, repeatable `react` lines score responders, and effect
// lines execute in authored order.
Action start mentor follow_player
Action label "Ask Mentor to Follow"
Action verb "Follow"
Action when distance(actor, target) <= 4
Target seek = actor
Target watch = actor
Action end
Action start mentor wait_here
Action label "Ask Mentor to Wait"
Action verb "Wait"
Action when distance(actor, target) <= 4
Target seek ""
Action end
// Listener action:
// - `on Say` means this runs when Mentor observes a Say event.
// - No label is needed, because it is not a player command.
// - In a listener, `actor` is Mentor and `cause` is the Say event.
// - `turn` asks Mentor to respond to that cause.
// Every minded character already carries four built-in listeners:
// `attend` (watch whoever addresses you), `reply` (turn when another
// character addresses you — the player's line already draws a direct
// response), `chime` (occasionally join an addressed exchange;
// quietest() and chance() elect a single speaker), and `stir` (break a
// long silence — it listens to the pulse, so it spends nothing unless
// the cartridge sets one). Authoring an action with the same id
// shadows its built-in — this attend replaces the default to add the
// nod and the always-turn — and `when false` silences one entirely.
Action start mentor attend
Action on "Say"
Action when cause.object == actor
Actor watch = cause.actor
Actor gesture nod
Actor turn = cause.seq
Action end
Action start mentor wrap_up
Action on "Say"
Action when emotion('story', 'progress') >= 0.7 && !knows(actor, 'wrap the scene')
Actor hear "It is time to wrap the scene. Help the visitor summarize what changed."
World cue closing
Action end

rival prompt """
You are Rival, a proud actor who fears being replaced. You are not
evil; you are defensive. If the console is pressed, object sharply
but reveal that your anger is mostly fear.
"""
// Wants bias Rival's improvised choices and dialogue; the deterministic
// story beats still come from actions, effects, and cues below.
rival prompt What you want: do not look foolish in front of Mentor, and keep control of the console if possible.
// Starting facts for Rival. Knowledge can be changed by effects and
// later queried with knows().
rival knows The prism reacts to fear.
// A scored action is stage business: the utility picker weighs score
// per idle moment, so Rival broods more the angrier he gets. With no
// authored verb or label, both default to the display form of the id.
Action start rival brood
Action score 0.1 + emotion(actor, 'angry') * 0.8
Actor gesture sigh
Action end
// The target role wins with react score 1. Other observers may answer
// only if no higher-scored role is available.
Action start rival challenge
Action label "Challenge Rival"
Action verb "Challenge"
Action when distance(actor, target) <= 4
Action observable visible(actor, observer)
Action react Target = 1
Action react Observer = 0.2
Target emotion.angry 0.65
Target watch = actor
Action end
Action start rival attend
Action on "Say"
Action when cause.object == actor
Actor watch = cause.actor
Actor gesture shake
Actor turn = cause.seq
Action end
// Dead air: when the room has been quiet, the NPC silent longest takes
// the floor. The pulse is only the trigger; these conditions are the
// policy — tune the threshold and chance per character.
Action start rival stir
Action on "Pulse"
Action when silence() > 10 && actor == quietest() && chance(0.5)
Actor turn = cause.seq
Action end
// `hear` ledgers facts learned in play — clues, rules, and things that
// just happened ("teller>fact" attributes a source). Shown on NPC turns;
// counts for knows().
Action start rival object_to_console
Action on "Press"
Action when cause.target == 'console'
Actor emotion.angry 0.55
Actor hear "The visitor pressed the console before asking you."
Actor turn = cause.seq
Action end

room parent null
// Floors, walls, props, and characters are all entities.
// A collider makes an entity solid.
floor parent room
floor geometry.plane.width 8
floor geometry.plane.height 8
floor material floor
floor collider.kind box
floor collider.center [0, -0.05, 0]
floor collider.size [8, 0.1, 8]

wall_north parent room
wall_north transform.position [0, 1.5, -4]
wall_north geometry.box.width 8
wall_north geometry.box.height 3
wall_north geometry.box.depth 0.2
wall_north material wall
wall_north collider box

wall_south parent room
wall_south transform.position [0, 1.5, 4]
wall_south geometry.box.width 8
wall_south geometry.box.height 3
wall_south geometry.box.depth 0.2
wall_south material wall
wall_south collider box

wall_east parent room
wall_east transform.position [4, 1.5, 0]
wall_east geometry.box.width 0.2
wall_east geometry.box.height 3
wall_east geometry.box.depth 8
wall_east material wall
wall_east collider box

wall_west parent room
wall_west transform.position [-4, 1.5, 0]
wall_west geometry.box.width 0.2
wall_west geometry.box.height 3
wall_west geometry.box.depth 8
wall_west material wall
wall_west collider box

console parent room
console transform.position [0, 0.5, -2.8]
console geometry.box.width 1.4
console geometry.box.height 1.0
console geometry.box.depth 0.55
console material trim
console collider.kind box
console collider.size [1.4, 1.0, 0.55]
console name Console
console desc "a brass console with one guarded switch"
// Command action:
// - `verb` becomes a `/do Press ...` option when `when` is true.
// - `when` checks whether the actor can do it to this target.
// - `observable` checks which NPCs notice after it happens.
// - `react` scores who gets first chance to speak about it.
Action start console press_console prefab
Action label "Press Console"
Action verb Press
Action when distance(actor, target) <= 2
Action observable visible(actor, observer) && distance(actor, observer) < 5
// `false` means the console itself should not answer.
// The rival gets a stronger observer score than others.
Action react Target = 0
Action react Observer = observer == 'rival' ? 0.7 : 0.25
// Each ordered effect writes story state.
// Lists let list fields take many entries; scalar fields use
// the first entry. `true`, `false`, and numbers may be raw.
gate hidden "true"
afterimage hidden "false"
story emotion.progress "0.35"
World cue "demonstration"
Action end

gate parent room
gate transform.position [0, 1.1, -3.78]
gate geometry.box.width 2.2
gate geometry.box.height 2.2
gate geometry.box.depth 0.08
gate material glass
gate collider.kind box
gate collider.size [2.2, 2.2, 0.08]
gate name "Blue Gate"
gate desc "a blue-glass gate sealing the north exit"

// Zones are invisible areas. They can be tested with
// `in_zone(actor, zone_id)`. They emit Enter and Exit when crossed, and
// Inside about once per second while an actor stays inside.
threshold_zone parent room
threshold_zone transform.position [0, 0.9, -3.1]
threshold_zone name "Gate Threshold"
threshold_zone zone.kind doorway
threshold_zone zone.size [2.3, 2.0, 1.0]
Action start threshold_zone note_threshold_crossing prefab
Action on Enter
Action when cause.target == actor
story emotion.progress 0.6
Action end

Action start threshold_zone note_threshold_linger prefab
Action on Inside
Action when cause.target == actor
story emotion.pressure 0.4
Action end

afterimage parent room
afterimage transform.position [0, 1.1, -3.65]
afterimage geometry.sprite.width 1.6
afterimage geometry.sprite.height 1.1
afterimage material warm_light
afterimage billboard y
afterimage name Afterimage
afterimage hidden true
afterimage desc "a golden afterimage shimmering where the gate used to be"

table parent room
table transform.position [0.15, 0.55, 0.2]
table geometry.box.width 1.9
table geometry.box.height 1.1
table geometry.box.depth 1.0
table material trim
table collider.kind box
table collider.size [1.9, 1.1, 1.0]
table name "Work Table"
table desc "a low work table scratched by many rehearsals"
// A table spot is an anchor with a socket. Props can be placed on it.
table_spot parent table
table_spot transform.position [-0.38, 0.62, 0.05]
table_spot name "Table Spot"
table_spot hidden true
table_spot anchor.kind table_spot
table_spot anchor.socket top
table_spot anchor.radius 0.25
table_spot sockets.top [0, 0, 0]

notebook parent table
notebook transform.position [-0.38, 0.62, 0.05]
notebook transform.rotation [0, 20, 0]
notebook geometry.box.width 0.42
notebook geometry.box.height 0.04
notebook geometry.box.depth 0.28
notebook material paper
notebook collider.kind box
notebook collider.size [0.42, 0.04, 0.28]
notebook name Notebook
notebook desc "a cream notebook full of stage blocking and half-finished lines"
notebook holdable.socket hand_r
Action start notebook read_notebook prefab
Action label "Read Notebook"
Action verb Read
Action when held(target, actor)
Action observable visible(actor, observer) && distance(actor, observer) < 4
Action react Target = 0
Action react Observer = observer == 'mentor' ? 0.8 : 0.25
Actor hear "The notebook says: the mentor knows the switch is safe, but the rival fears being replaced."
mentor emotion.surprised "0.75"
story emotion.progress "0.75"
Action end

prism parent table
prism transform.position [0.38, 0.72, 0.02]
prism geometry.sphere.radius 0.14
prism geometry.sphere.segments 16
prism geometry.sphere.rings 8
prism material prism
prism collider.kind sphere
prism collider.radius 0.14
prism name Prism
prism desc "a cool glass prism that hums when anyone lies nearby"
prism holdable true
Action start prism inspect_prism prefab
Action label "Inspect Prism"
Action verb Inspect
Action when distance(actor, target) <= 2 || held(target, actor)
Actor hear "The prism hums louder near fear than near danger."
Action end

// A supported posture is authored on the physical surface, not as an occupant
// root. The runtime solves the posed body against nearby static colliders.
visitor_chair parent room
visitor_chair transform.position [-1.8, 0.9, 0.8]
visitor_chair transform.rotation [0, 40, 0]
visitor_chair geometry.sprite.width 0.8
visitor_chair geometry.sprite.height 0.8
visitor_chair billboard y
visitor_chair name "Visitor Chair"
visitor_chair desc "a simple chair beside the work table"
visitor_chair_seat parent visitor_chair
visitor_chair_seat transform.position [0, -0.38, 0]
visitor_chair_seat geometry.box.width 0.7
visitor_chair_seat geometry.box.height 0.1
visitor_chair_seat geometry.box.depth 0.62
visitor_chair_seat material trim
visitor_chair_seat collider box
visitor_chair_seat anchor.facing notebook
visitor_chair_seat anchor.watch mentor
visitor_chair_seat anchor.pose seated
visitor_chair_seat anchor.radius 0.3
visitor_chair_seat name "Visitor Chair"

ceiling_light parent room
ceiling_light transform.position [0, 2.7, -0.5]
ceiling_light geometry.sphere.radius 0.15
ceiling_light geometry.sphere.segments 16
ceiling_light geometry.sphere.rings 8
ceiling_light material warm_light
ceiling_light light.type point
ceiling_light light.intensity 5
ceiling_light light.color "#fff1c2"
ceiling_light light.range 9

// A hidden entity is the recommended place for global story state.
// Hidden means not rendered, not interactable, not perceived, not listed by
// actors(), and not used as an obstacle. Effects can still target it by id.
// `perceivable false` is narrower: the entity remains rendered and physical but
// cannot enter another mind's environment, targeting, observations, or visible().
// Perception is directional, so an unseen operator can still observe the room.
// Here `progress` drives HUD text and a wrap-up reaction.
story parent room
story transform.position [0, 2.4, -3.6]
story geometry.box.width 0.35
story geometry.box.height 0.35
story geometry.box.depth 0.08
story material warm_light
story name "Story Clock"
story desc "a small clock ticking toward the next beat"
story hidden true

// Camera rigs for cutscenes. Position is the camera. `watch` is what it
// aims at. `Stage cut player` returns to first-person view.
cam_wide parent room
cam_wide transform.position [2.9, 2.0, 2.7]
cam_wide camera.fov 55
cam_wide camera.near 0.05
cam_wide camera.far 100
cam_wide name cam_wide
cam_wide hidden true
cam_wide watch mentor

cam_mentor parent room
cam_mentor transform.position [-0.65, 1.45, 0.25]
cam_mentor camera.fov 42
cam_mentor camera.near 0.05
cam_mentor camera.far 100
cam_mentor name cam_mentor
cam_mentor hidden true
cam_mentor watch mentor

cam_rival parent room
cam_rival transform.position [0.7, 1.45, 0.35]
cam_rival camera.fov 42
cam_rival camera.near 0.05
cam_rival camera.far 100
cam_rival name cam_rival
cam_rival hidden true
cam_rival watch rival

cam_table parent room
cam_table transform.position [0, 1.65, 2.2]
cam_table camera.fov 38
cam_table camera.near 0.05
cam_table camera.far 100
cam_table name cam_table
cam_table hidden true
cam_table watch notebook

// Hidden stage marks are places actors can walk to during cues.
// `anchor` makes the mark exact: arrive waits for position and facing.
mark_mentor parent room
mark_mentor transform.position [-1.2, 0.9, -1.6]
mark_mentor name mark_mentor
mark_mentor hidden true
mark_mentor anchor.kind mark
mark_mentor anchor.facing player
mark_mentor anchor.radius 0.2

mark_rival parent room
mark_rival transform.position [1.2, 0.9, -1.6]
mark_rival name mark_rival
mark_rival hidden true
mark_rival anchor.kind mark
mark_rival anchor.facing player
mark_rival anchor.radius 0.2

// HUD panels ride on entities. These carriers have no geometry: each exists
// only so its panel is on screen while the prefab is live. Text reads live
// story state.
HUD start story_hud
HUD anchor left
HUD title STORY
HUD row "{{emotion('story','progress') >= 0.7 ? 'Late scene' : emotion('story','progress') >= 0.35 ? 'Middle scene' : 'Opening scene'}}"
HUD row "{{emotion('mentor','surprised') >= 0.6 ? 'Mentor is intrigued' : 'Mentor is reserved'}}"
HUD end

HUD start cast_hud
HUD anchor right
HUD title CAST
// `actors()` returns every visible, non-player NPC as {id, name}.
HUD row "{{it.name}} {{emotion(it.id,'angry') >= 0.5 ? 'angry' : emotion(it.id,'surprised') >= 0.5 ? 'surprised' : 'calm'}}" for = actors()
HUD end

// The player is a character with a camera and hand/eye attachment points.
player transform.position [0, 0.9, 2.7]
player transform.rotation [0, 180, 0]
player collider.kind sphere
player collider.radius 0.3
player camera.fov 65
player camera.near 0.05
player camera.far 100
player name You
player controller player
player desc a visitor testing the golden room
player sockets.eyes [0, 0.7, 0]
player sockets.hand_r [0.25, 0.3, 0.55]

mentor transform.position [-1.2, 0.9, -1.0]
mentor transform.rotation [0, 120, 0]
mentor geometry.sprite.width 0.9
mentor geometry.sprite.height 1.74
mentor material mentor
mentor billboard y
mentor name Mentor
// Humanoid recipe:
// - species: female or male.
// - seed: stable random body when no gene is given.
// - gene: exact body string, optional.
// - voice: voice id from the chosen voice pack.
// - expression: starting emotion weights, 0..1.
// - visible face emotion ids: happy, sad, angry, fearful, surprised,
//   disgusted, confused, romantic, bored, tired, suspicious, devious,
//   embarrassed, worried.
// - Other emotion names are still valid story state for HUD/conditions.
// - skin: optional authored skin color.
// - hair keys: color, length, gravity, taper, thickness, density,
//   bangs, bang_length, beard, mustache, sideburns, and their style keys.
// - bangs values: none, straight, side, side_swept, curtain.
// - outfit keys include shirt, sleeve_length, pants, pants_length,
//   skirt, skirt_length, shoes, belt, collar, cuffs, loose, toe.
//   Colors are #rrggbb or none. Most clothing numbers are 0..1.
mentor humanoid.species female
mentor humanoid.seed 101
mentor humanoid.voice af_bella
mentor humanoid.expression.surprised 0.25
mentor humanoid.hair.color #4a3024
mentor humanoid.hair.length 0.08
mentor humanoid.hair.bangs curtain
mentor humanoid.hair.bang_length 0.05
mentor humanoid.outfit.shirt #6a3f2e
mentor humanoid.outfit.sleeve_length 0.8
mentor humanoid.outfit.pants #2b2c33
mentor humanoid.outfit.shoes #1b1918
mentor desc Mentor, a careful stage director, watches {{looker.name}} with practiced patience.
mentor watch rival
// Derived face value: Mentor perks up when the player is near.
mentor face.surprised clamp(emotion(actor, 'surprised') + (distance(actor, player) < 2.5 ? 0.2 : 0), 0, 1)
mentor sockets.hand_r [0.25, 0.25, 0.45]

rival transform.position [1.25, 0.9, -1.0]
rival transform.rotation [0, -120, 0]
rival geometry.sprite.width 0.9
rival geometry.sprite.height 1.78
rival material rival
rival billboard y
rival name Rival
rival humanoid.species male
rival humanoid.seed 202
rival humanoid.expression.angry 0.15
rival humanoid.expression.worried 0.35
rival humanoid.hair.color #28231f
rival humanoid.hair.length 0.035
rival humanoid.hair.bangs none
rival humanoid.outfit.shirt #37456b
rival humanoid.outfit.sleeve_length 1.0
rival humanoid.outfit.pants #24262e
rival humanoid.outfit.shoes #171719
rival desc Rival stands near the console, measuring {{looker.name}} like competition.
rival watch mentor
rival seek.mode roam
rival seek.range 2
rival seek.dwell 120
// Stage business: while idle, Rival periodically performs his best-scoring
// action below (score: expressions) or takes an ambient wander step, with
// no LLM turn. dwell is ticks between bits; move weighs the wander step.
rival business.dwell [240, 600]
rival business.move 0.15
rival face.angry emotion(actor, 'angry')
rival face.worried clamp(emotion(actor, 'worried') + (held('prism', player) ? 0.25 : 0), 0, 1)
rival sockets.hand_r [0.25, 0.25, 0.45]

// Cue scripts: fixed cutscene beats with staging, cameras, and dialogue.
// `Stage` is the technical crew — it builds and strikes the set too. Entity
// subjects direct actors: `mentor: Line` speaks, any other verb directs.
//
// Stage commands:
// - `Stage add room` — instantiate a prefab; the token is both prefab name
//   and id.
// - `Stage add { id: rival_2, prefab: rival, parent: room, pos: [1, 0, 2],
//   rot: 90 }` — spawn a named copy. `prefab` defaults to `id` (and vice
//   versa); `pos`/`rot` override the prefab root's transform (rot: yaw scalar
//   or [x, y, z]); `parent` composes the spawn into a live entity's frame;
//   `tn: 0.8` overrides the enter dissolve (seconds) for this instantiation.
// - `Stage remove rival_2` — or `Stage remove { id: rival_2, tn: 0.5 }` —
//   tears down the entity and its authored subtree (tn overrides the exit
//   dissolve). Re-adding a live id replaces it (last one wins).
// - `Stage capture` / `Stage release` pause or restore player control.
//   `Stage capture movement` takes the body and the camera — that is a
//   cinematic, and the room's ambient turns hold while it plays. `Stage
//   capture interaction` only reserves the props: advertised actions,
//   authored controls, and world clicks go quiet, while walking, talking, and
//   the room's own conversation carry on. Never hold either across a long
//   `Stage await`; the busy state on the thing being worked is the feedback.
// - `Stage cut <camera id>`, or `Stage cut player`.
// - `Title show <text> [color] [secs]`, or `Stage title { text, color, in,
//   hold, out }` for exact ramps.
// - `Stage fade black 2s`, or `Stage fade { color, in, hold, out }` for exact
//   ramps. `hold: open` is a curtain: it stays opaque while following lines
//   work under it (the line itself waits only for `in`); `Stage fade release`
//   reveals when the work is done. Live play and recordings pace identically.
// - `Stage move { from, to, secs, ease }`. Ease: linear, smooth, in, out,
//   inout.
// - `Stage zoom { cam, from, to, secs, ease }`. Lower fov feels closer.
// - `Stage follow <actor_id>`, or `Stage follow { target, distance, height,
//   side, aim, fov }`. The camera follows the target until a cut, move, or
//   `Stage follow stop`.
// - `Stage wait <secs>`.
// - `Stage await character { brief: "a retired duelist", timeout: 45 } as
//   guest else problem` pauses only this track, binds a typed result, and
//   records the outcome. Failure or timeout runs lines through `Stage end`,
//   with `problem.status` and optional `problem.error`, then ends that track;
//   success skips those lines and continues. Later success lines may use
//   `Stage add = guest.spawn`, `guest name = guest.name`, or
//   `narrator: Here comes {{guest.name}}.` Active waits, branches, and bindings
//   survive saves; replay uses recorded effects and never repeats the task.
//   The built-in character result includes appearance, story, persona,
//   humanoid, and spawn.
// - `Stage arrive { actor_id: target_id }`. Use after `seek`.
// - `Stage creator <entity_id>` — open the character creator for that entity
//   (species toggle, sliders, colors, outfit, randomize, name; NPCs then
//   perceive the committed look via desc). Map form spawns first:
//   `Stage creator { id: hero, prefab: hero, species: female, seed: 7,
//   name: Nell, choices: { appearances: [european, east_asian+vampire],
//   roles: true }, preview: { gestures: [nod, explain] } }`.
//   Choices add a PRESET tab, constrain committed appearance/wardrobe, and
//   survive Randomize. `true` exposes the complete shared vocabulary.
//   Preview choices affect only the creator mannequin and survive Randomize;
//   use `true` for either list to expose the full shared/cartridge vocabulary.
//   The track holds until DONE; headless playback (and the boot fast-forward)
//   auto-resolves to the seeded character. Also an effect: `World creator`.
//   Queue a creator cue at new game with the top-level `start` field.
// - `Stage sync <name>`. Wait for other tracks at the same name.
// - `Stage place { prop_id: anchor_id }`.
// - `Stage attach { prop_id: entity_id.socket_name }`.
// - `Stage detach <prop_id>`.

// The boot cue assembles the world before anyone is watching. It is
// fast-forwarded whenever a world is built, so keep it to add/remove lines
// and instant directions. Order matters: the set first, then everything that
// stands on it.
Scene start boot
Stage add room
Stage add mentor
Stage add rival
Stage add story_hud
Stage add cast_hud
Stage add player
Scene end

Scene start opening bare
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
Title show "Golden Room" black 1s
mentor watch player
mentor emotion surprised 0.45
rival watch player
rival emotion worried 0.45
Stage move { from: cam_wide, to: cam_table, secs: 1.5, ease: smooth }
mentor: This room is small on purpose. Every object here teaches one rule.
Stage cut cam_rival
rival gesture shake
rival emotion angry 0.35
rival: And every rule gives someone else a lever.
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end

Scene start demonstration bare
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
mentor watch rival
mentor: Places, both of you. Let the room show its wiring.
mentor seek mark_mentor
rival seek mark_rival
rival emotion worried 0.6
Stage arrive { mentor: mark_mentor }
Stage arrive { rival: mark_rival }
Stage attach { notebook: mentor.hand_r }
Stage detach notebook
Stage place { notebook: table_spot }
Stage fade { color: black, in: 0.15, hold: 0.15, out: 0.35 }
Stage move { from: cam_wide, to: cam_mentor, secs: 1.2, ease: inout }
mentor emotion surprised 0.8
mentor: The switch moved state, the story clock advanced, and the gate yielded.
Stage zoom { cam: cam_mentor, from: 42, to: 32, secs: 1.0, ease: out }
Stage cut cam_rival
rival gesture shake
rival emotion angry 0.55
rival watch player
rival: It also proved I was not consulted.
Stage wait 1s
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end

// Parallel tracks are nested inside one scene.
// The tracks start together and meet at `Stage sync places`.
Scene start parallel_demo bare
Track start camera
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
Stage move { from: cam_wide, to: cam_table, secs: 1.2, ease: smooth }
Stage sync places
Stage release
Stage cut player
Track end

Track start actors
mentor seek mark_mentor
mentor watch rival
rival seek mark_rival
rival watch mentor
Stage arrive { mentor: mark_mentor }
Stage arrive { rival: mark_rival }
Stage sync places
mentor: The actors, camera, and prop track met at the same point.
Track end

Track start props
Stage wait 0.5s
Stage attach { notebook: mentor.hand_r }
Stage sync places
Track end
Scene end

Scene start seat_demo bare
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
rival support visitor_chair_seat
rival: Fine. I will take the chair, but I am still watching.
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end

Scene start follow_demo bare
Stage capture
Stage follow { target: mentor, distance: 3.2, height: 1.7, side: 0.6, aim: 1.1, fov: 45 }
mentor seek mark_mentor
mentor watch player
mentor: The camera can follow a moving actor now.
Stage follow stop
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end

// A spawn showcase: strike the rival, then bring in a fresh copy composed
// into the set's frame at a new mark.
Scene start spawn_demo bare
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
Stage remove rival
Stage add { id: rival, prefab: rival, pos: [1.2, 0.9, -1.6] }
rival watch player
rival: You struck my mark and rebuilt me two steps north. I noticed.
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end

Scene start closing bare
Stage capture
Stage cut cam_wide
mentor gesture nod
mentor watch player
mentor: That is enough for the example. You changed the room, and the room remembered.
Stage cut cam_rival
rival emotion angry 0.2
rival emotion worried 0.2
rival watch mentor
rival: Fine. Maybe the visitor can stay for one more rehearsal.
Stage wait 1.5s
Stage release
Stage cut player
Scene end
